The statistical comparison reveals two complementary findings. First, HQI-SAC-Fed is highly significantly superior to the independent SAC baseline: $t(38) = 14.82$, $p = 2.1 \times 10^{-11}$, Cohen's $d = 4.67$ (very large effect). This confirms that federation provides substantial coordination benefit (+32\% NPV versus independent operation). Second, HQI-SAC-Fed is statistically indistinguishable from the centralised SAC upper bound: $t(38) = 0.71$, $p = 0.48$ (non-significant), Cohen's $d = 0.22$ (negligible effect). This non-significant result is intentionally positive: it demonstrates that federated learning with differential privacy achieves performance statistically equivalent to the privacy-violating centralised benchmark, validating the core thesis that coordination can be achieved without data sharing.

Wind curtailment is reduced from approximately 32\% (no-BESS baseline) to 8.3\% with HQI-SAC-Fed ($-$23.7 percentage points), saving 284{,}000\,MWh/year and avoiding \$6.8M\,CAD/year in curtailment-related losses. CO\textsubscript{2} emissions are reduced by 162\,kt/year---within 1.8\% of the centralised benchmark's 165\,kt/year.

\subsection{Convergence Analysis}
\label{sec:convergence}

Fig.~\ref{fig:convergence} presents the federation convergence trajectory. HQI-SAC-Fed converges at round~82 of 100 (defined as $<$1\% NPV improvement over 10 consecutive rounds), reaching \$13.2M NPV. The centralised SAC---which observes all sites' data---converges more rapidly (round~$\sim$45) but plateaus at a comparable level (\$13.6M). Independent SAC converges earliest (round~$\sim$35) but to a substantially lower NPV (\$10.0M), reflecting the coordination deficit.

